{
  "id": "80554",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Factors Affecting Reaction Rates",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The rates at which reactants are consumed and products are formed during chemical reactions vary greatly. We can identify five factors that affect the rates of chemical reactions: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "krefsx07qo347y4",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Chemical nature of the reacting substances"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "jpuc3i7b6mdyr42",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3611108577",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "State of subdivision (one large lump versus many small particles) of the reactants"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2zd05b0iip2al6a",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3735375732",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Temperature"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "ch001keexte3e7v",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3102885269",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Concentrations of the reactants"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3w3evtplrneueji",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3150303781",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Presence of a "
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "catalyst"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "A substance that increases the rate of a reaction without itself being consumed by the reaction."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "2633124496",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "1852781662",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "pn46qlzsc0rp4ch",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2374910106",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1084181464",
            "style": "decimal",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "8mxuctrgn7mmhgw",
                "text": "The Chemical Nature of the Reacting Substances"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1ajbw9eomlx7eco",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The rate of a reaction depends on the nature of the participating substances. Reactions that appear similar may occur at different rates for different reactants under the same conditions. For example, when small pieces of the metals sodium and iron are exposed to air, the sodium reacts completely with air overnight, whereas the iron is barely affected. The active metals calcium and sodium both react with water to form hydrogen gas and a base. Yet calcium reacts at a moderate rate, whereas sodium reacts so rapidly that the reaction is almost explosive."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4zesy91ap19n1d6",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "lwwjkqu7qjry3bs",
                "text": "The State of Subdivision of the Reactants"
              }
            ],
            "id": "kvxk9ay3tywibpy",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Except for substances in the gaseous state or in solution, reactions occur at the boundary, or interface, between two "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "phases"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A phase is a distinct region of material with uniform chemical and physical properties."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4074911421",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3862393971",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Therefore, the rate of a reaction between two phases depends to a great extent on the surface contact between them. A finely divided solid has more surface area available for reaction than does one large piece of the same substance. Consequently, a liquid reacts more rapidly with a finely divided solid than with a large piece of the same solid. For example, large pieces of iron react slowly with acids, while finely divided iron reacts much more rapidly. Large pieces of wood smolder, smaller pieces burn rapidly, and sawdust burns explosively."
              }
            ],
            "id": "mchpcg7b5r890d4",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "This figure shows two photos labeled (a) and (b). Photo (a) shows the bottom of a test tube. The test tube is filled with a dark \n                    gas, and there is a dark substance and bubbles in the bottom. Photo (b) shows a rod and bubbles in a test tube similar to photo (a), \n                    but the gas in the test tube is not as dark.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Iron reacts with hydrochloric acid, producing hydrogen gas:"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " 2 Fe("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "s"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") + 6 HCl("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "aq"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") → 2 FeCl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "aq"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") + 3 H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "g"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Iron powder reacts rapidly because the powder has a large total surface area.  "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1084413965",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " An iron nail reacts much more slowly because it has a much smaller total surface area."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2584393716",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "pp83h4fz0wrf3rf",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "47patr35stpv4js",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/56/56ce2ae4a095582f92fc3e40f88be287/CNX_Chem_12_02_AcidDissol.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "jo1sltu2r5pojtz",
                "text": "Temperature"
              }
            ],
            "id": "mjg97xjkwwxozh7",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Chemical reactions typically occur faster at higher temperatures. Food can spoil quickly when left on the kitchen counter. However, the lower temperature inside a refrigerator slows that process so that the same food remains fresh for days. We use a burner or a hot plate in the laboratory to increase the speed of reactions that proceed slowly at ordinary temperatures. In many cases, an increase in temperature of only 10 °C will approximately double the rate of a reaction in a homogeneous system."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ud1ezwr7jaeyrsf",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "iq6p7f8bjwy2xqv",
                "text": "Concentrations of the Reactants"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r3msekxjbc1pwyz",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The rates of many reactions depend on the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "concentrations"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A quantitative measure of the relative amounts of solute and solvent present in a solution."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "542103745",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "329464432",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the reactants. Rates usually increase when the concentration of one or more of the reactants increases. For example, calcium carbonate (CaCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ") deteriorates as a result of its reaction with the pollutant sulfur dioxide. The rate of this reaction depends on the amount of sulfur dioxide in the air. An acidic oxide, sulfur dioxide combines with water vapor in the air to produce sulfurous acid in the following reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "985s7z08t6jen2y",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "2fcnb31ac91pr6k",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3ycqs3vfl41bmyo",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{SO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(g)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_3(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Calcium carbonate reacts with sulfurous acid as follows:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "499opuxieqcgd42",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "l6vztqzrw2lkurz",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "c2mhoi6ouqmqxgg",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{CaCO}}_3(s)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_3(aq)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;{\\mathrm{CaSO}}_3(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;+\\; {\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In a polluted atmosphere where the concentration of sulfur dioxide is high, calcium carbonate deteriorates more rapidly than in less polluted air. Similarly, phosphorus burns much more rapidly in an atmosphere of pure oxygen than in air, which is only about 20% oxygen."
              }
            ],
            "id": "v3gyvg4sn1msku6",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A photograph is shown of an angel statue. While some details of the statue, \n                    including facial features, are present, effects of weathering appear to be diminishing these features.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Statues made from carbonate compounds such as limestone and marble typically weather slowly over time due to the actions of water and of thermal expansion and contraction. However, pollutants like sulfur dioxide can accelerate weathering. As the concentration of air pollutants increases, deterioration of limestone occurs more rapidly. (Credit: James P Fisher III.)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "0grr8nndgprp7s7",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1hpr5i2n15c8tk6",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/e1/e1d7c250e321fd4fd7b9167e0c24fb2b/CNX_Chem_12_02_NYSStatue.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "400"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "sgxcwa5q4fh0tjg",
                "text": "The Presence of a Catalyst"
              }
            ],
            "id": "jf78wgjtlegxg0j",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction by lowering the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "activation energy"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The minimum energy molecules need to react; the height of the energy barrier to formation of products."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1017105653",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2851003582",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " without itself being consumed by the reaction. Activation energy is the minimum amount of energy required for a chemical reaction to proceed. As an example, hydrogen peroxide solutions foam when poured onto an open wound because substances in the exposed tissues act as catalysts, increasing the rate of hydrogen peroxide’s decomposition. However, in the absence of catalysts (such as when the hydrogen peroxide is stored in a bottle in the medicine cabinet), complete decomposition can take months. A catalyst increases the reaction rate by providing an alternative pathway or mechanism for the reaction to follow. "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Catalysis"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The increase in rate of a chemical reaction due to the addition of a catalyst. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "304480163",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "955693403",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " as it relates to mechanisms of reactions is discussed in greater detail later in this unit."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3hlvx9k6oyyzoge",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A graph is shown with the label, “Reaction coordinate,” on the x-axis. The x-axis is depicted as an arrow. The y-axis is also an \n                    arrow and is labeled, “Energy.” There is a horizontal line that runs the width of the graph and appears just above the x-axis. A segment \n                    of this line is blue and is labeled, “Reactants.” From the right end of this line segment, a solid black, concave down curve is shown \n                    which reaches the level just below the end of the y-axis. The curve ends at another short, blue line labeled, “Products.” The “Products” \n                    line appears at a higher level than the “Reactants” line. An arrow extends from the horizontal line to the apex of the curve. The arrow \n                    is labeled, “Uncatalyzed reaction activation energy.” A second, black concave down curve is shown. This curve also meets the reactants \n                    and products blue line segments, but only extends to about two-thirds the height of the initial curve. From the horizontal line is \n                    another arrow pointing to the apex of the second curve. This arrow is labeled, “Catalyzed reaction activation energy.”",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The presence of a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction by lowering its activation energy."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "j2p083hpnm8ytlt",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "d8wmwllnv02krth",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/62/622b9a68ae4ce6c9027af2346c99abe3/CNX_Chem_12_02_Coordinate.jpg",
